   ISSCIP 6 ), CHICAGO, IL, SEP 16-22, 1992},
Organization = {NATL SCI FDN; NATO; ARGONNE NATL LAB; UNIV CHICAGO, CHEM DEPT; DOW; FORD
   MOTOR; IBM; THREE M CO; AMOCO; EXXON},
Abstract = {Photoexcitation and photoionization experiments on small Tungsten and
   Niobium clusters were performed with a Q-switched Nd:YAG laser (1064 nm)
   and a reflectron type time-of-flight mass spectrometer. For low laser
   fluences the monomer and very small clusters do not show up in the mass
   spectra. Furthermore, the detected cluster ions show very asymmetric
   peak shapes caused by delayed ionization (thermionic emission). For high
   photon fluences photoions with up to charge state +3 could be detected.},
